WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.371
Tools for long tasks: small results, clear next steps.

2
00:00:03.651 --> 00:00:06.659
Studio 1 covered tool design for single calls.

3
00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:15.856
Long tasks raise the stakes, because every tool result stays in the context for the rest of the run and every token is read again on each later step.

4
00:00:16.136 --> 00:00:21.917
Anthropic’s “Writing effective tools for AI agents” gives the guidance that matters most here.

5
00:00:22.197 --> 00:00:23.371
Search, don’t list.

6
00:00:23.651 --> 00:00:29.091
A list_documents tool that returns 400 entries makes the agent read all of them.

7
00:00:29.371 --> 00:00:36.325
A search_docs(query) tool that returns the five best matches with short snippets gives it what it needs to choose.

8
00:00:36.605 --> 00:00:38.291
Return high-signal fields.

9
00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:44.139
Titles, short snippets, and natural identifiers beat raw records full of internal ids.

10
00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:51.907
The post reports that resolving cryptic identifiers into meaningful names improved precision in retrieval tasks.

11
00:00:52.187 --> 00:00:54.427
Paginate and truncate, and say so.

12
00:00:54.707 --> 00:01:07.528
A read_doc(doc_id, start_line, max_lines) tool that returns 40 lines plus “more lines follow; call again with start_line=41” lets the agent read only as far as it needs.

13
00:01:07.808 --> 00:01:16.725
The post recommends sensible defaults for pagination, filtering, and truncation, and suggests telling the agent how to get more when you truncate.

14
00:01:17.005 --> 00:01:23.384
It also describes a response_format option that lets the agent choose concise or detailed output.

15
00:01:23.664 --> 00:01:25.797
Shorten results before you first send them.

16
00:01:26.077 --> 00:01:29.128
It is tempting to send full results and trim them later.

17
00:01:29.408 --> 00:01:38.155
On current Claude models that is costly: the next lesson explains why editing an old tool result on the client invalidates later reasoning blocks.

18
00:01:38.435 --> 00:01:41.101
Decide the size of a result when you create it.

19
00:01:41.381 --> 00:01:42.491
Make errors steer.

20
00:01:42.771 --> 00:01:44.989
“Document not found” is a dead end.

21
00:01:45.269 --> 00:01:47.275
“No document with id ‘risk-log’.

22
00:01:47.555 --> 00:01:54.061
Ids look like ‘risks-2026q3’; call search_docs to find one” is a next step.

23
00:01:54.341 --> 00:01:59.269
Finally, keep read and write tools separate and make the kind obvious from the name.

24
00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:05.757
That separation is what lets you run reads in parallel and gate writes, which week 4 covers in depth.
